[Music]  to get started with the cloud natural  language API for Python  you need a GCP project which you can  setup in the console  in your project enable the google  natural language API for that project  then create a service account and  download the private key as Jason you  use these credentials to access google  cloud api's so keep it secure and out of  your code and public repos  to access the credentials from your  project set up an environment variable  I'm going to use PyCharm as my IDE so  the way I set up a variable is different  I'll show you that shortly if you aren't  using pycharm go to the terminal and set  an environment variable to the path of  your service account prepare your  environment for Python development again  I'm using PyCharm but you can use  whatever dev environment you prefer if  you are using PyCharm then under run  select edit configurations in the  section environment there's an  environment variable field click on this  and add the Google application  credentials here next install the client  library from the terminal or IDE if you  use cloud shell in the console you can  skip this set since the required  dependencies are already included make a  new file or open an existing file import  the Google cloud client library and then  instantiate a client  provide some text to analyze I'm going  to go with a classic example here now  create a document which has a field  called content set to the text you are  analyzing and a field called type which  as the name suggests is the type of text  in this case plain text call analyze  sentiment passing the document this  function returns an analyze sentiment  response analyze sentiment response has  three properties document sentiment the  overall sentiment of the input document  language the language of the text and  sentences the sentiment for all the  sentences in the document set the  sentiment variable to the document  sentiment log the parameters of  sentiment score and magnitude scores a  sentiment score between negative 1 for  negative sentiment and positive 1 for  positive sentiment magnitude is a number  between 0 and infinity which represents  the absolute magnitude of the sentiment  regardless of positive or negative score  ok go ahead and run the code there you  go you've just sent your first request  to the natural language API but there's  so much more that the cloud natural  language API can do like analyzing  syntax and annotating text to find out  more about the client libraries consult  natural language basics or work through  the sentiment analysis tutorial check  out the cloud documentation  you

